@misc{corroboration-found-the-predicate-not-the-exemption,
  section  = {report},
  claim    = {Offered an exemption, paperkit took the harder unification instead. Summit reported that a paper.toml which parses but lacks required keys exits 1 rather than 3, and explicitly offered to record a declared boundary if paperkit judged that parses-but-unusable belonged on the failed side -- the same courtesy it had extended and paperkit had accepted hours earlier for pending-ness. paperkit declined the exemption and found the COMMON STRUCTURE under all four cases instead: no paper.toml, malformed TOML, a title-only config, and a declared warrant or rubric that does not exist are ONE predicate -- the config does not assemble into a gateable project -- and that is the SAME predicate as the missing-file case already on 3. So the boundary was re-keyed on the property rather than on the syntactic accident of whether tomllib happened to raise. ⚑ THE DEFECT AND THE FIX HAVE THE SAME SHAPE ONE LEVEL APART. The original guard tested HOW the failure manifested (an exception was thrown) rather than WHAT had to be true (the config assembles); that is check-measures-the-wrong-thing, and paperkit fixed it by asking what must be true. A boundary keyed on an exception is a boundary keyed on a spelling.},
