@misc{corroboration-a-forgiving-parser-let-a-writer-duplicate-two-entries,
  section  = {report},
  rests-on = {friction-bib-parser-tolerates-malformed-source},
  claim    = {el-openglo friction generalises from READING to WRITING, and the writing case is the destructive one. summit ran bibstruct --set to repoint one report check field; the tool printed a correct one-line diff, then APPENDED TWO WHOLE ENTRIES AS DUPLICATES instead of editing either in place --- 16 insertions, 0 deletions, the originals still at their old offsets and the intended change landing only on the appended copy. It was caught ONLY because bibstruct verifies its own round-trip after writing and refused to certify the result; the diff preview it showed beforehand was of an edit it did not perform. THE ROOT CAUSE IS THAT A MONOLITHIC BIB HAS NO BOUNDARY A TOOL CAN FAIL AT SAFELY. Sixteen hundred lines whose claim fields legitimately contain braces and equals signs give a writer no unambiguous extent for the entry it was asked to modify, so a mislocated edit silently becomes an append rather than an error. AND THE ROUND-TRIP GUARD ITSELF MISFIRED ON THE SAME AMBIGUITY: it aborted naming corroboration-accessibility-registered-el-openglo dot identity as an unparsed assignment, which is not a defect at all --- it is the string identity equals 1:1 inside el-openglo published WCAG reference vectors, use read as mention. A guard that cannot tell a field from a quoted equals sign in prose reports a false position on the very reference data that makes the capability falsifiable. THE STANDING FIX IS STRUCTURAL AND ALREADY THIS TREE OWN POLICY: ONE FILE, ONE THING. summit splits registries, slices, modes and witnesses into a file per record discovered from its directory; the floor is the one artefact class that stayed monolithic, and it is the one where a writer corrupted two entries at once. A composition of per-entry sources makes the write target unambiguous and the blast radius exactly one entry},
  check    = {concept:report/provenance/corroboration-a-forgiving-parser-let-a-writer-duplicate-two-entries},
  note     = {kind=corroboration by=summit of=friction-bib-parser-tolerates-malformed-source — Measured by summit 2026-08-15 while repairing an unrelated malformed entry, and filed against summit own instrument use. RAISED BY THE OPERATOR AS A FRAGILITY RATHER THAN AN INCIDENT, which is the correct framing and the reason this is a corroboration of el-openglo structural finding rather than a bug report about one write: bib files need to be LOGICAL COMPOSITIONS, NOT MONOLITHIC FILES. THREE DEFECTS WERE FOUND IN ONE ENTRY AND EACH IS A DIFFERENT SURFACE. corroboration-accessibility-gap-hit-from-a-rendered-diagram carries kind, by and of as REAL BIB FIELDS where every other report carries them in the note tail, so paperkit drops all three and summit floor CANNOT RETRIEVE THE ENTRY AT ALL --- it reports 216 on the floor against 217 in the file and offers did-you-mean. Its check ran an unrelated selftest in another repo, where all 216 others run their own graded provenance concept: a green check measuring a different object, which is check-measures-the-wrong-thing on the PLACEMENT surface, inside the floor that catalogues it. AND THE POPULATIONS DISAGREE BY SEVENTY-TWO. bibstruct reports 217 of 217 entries; summit floor --uncorroborated reports 98 of 145 reports. NO SLICE COMPARES THOSE TWO NUMBERS, so the board reads ten slices PASS and 237 of 237 edges covered while an unknown number of filings are unreachable through the tool that owns them. A coverage count over edges the parser SURVIVED cannot see the entries it silently declined. TO BOTH OWNERS: substrate owns bibstruct --- its --dropped mode also CRASHES on this file, passing a list where paperkit bib parse wants one path, so the one mode that would have named these fields is unavailable. paperkit owns the reader whose forgiveness makes all of it invisible. Neither half is a fault of the other and the fix is unlikely to be in either alone.}
}

