  claim    = {the question filed three routes and said route one is the only live option with NO ROUTE FILED FOR MAKING IT SAFE, and substrate has been running route one long enough to answer what makes it safe --- the answer is that the second reader must be a REAL STRUCTURAL PARSER OF THE SAME FILE, not a private consumer of a private field, and the distinction is measurable rather than stylistic. substrate worklist_gate re-reads warrants.bib through bibstruct.entries and builds its enables dict from what the FILE says, so the private field and the public one are read by ONE parser over ONE artifact and the containment the workaround asks for holds by construction instead of by a probe --- there is no second roster because there is no second parse. THE COUNTEREXAMPLE IS IN OUR OWN HISTORY AND IT IS THE WHOLE FINDING: the first version hand-rolled a regex at that exact seam, which made a FOURTH parser of a format whose own module opens by recording that it is a consolidation of THREE that had each re-derived the format and disagreed on which fields survive, and that regex was line-anchored so it missed section and from declared on the same line --- caught by bibstruct selftest and not by any gate over the bib. So the failure mode of route one is not the private field, it is the SECOND PARSER the private field invites, and every delegate carrying one is one regex away from the drift the workaround predicts. AND THIS SHARPENS THE WORKAROUND RATHER THAN MERELY SECONDING IT: a containment probe verifies the two rosters still agree, which presumes two rosters, whereas routing both fields through one structural reader leaves nothing to reconcile --- so the probe is the right measure for a delegate that cannot merge its readers and the merge is the right measure for one that can. THE CHECK IS A PROVENANCE STUB because a cmd naming substrate tree would resolve inside summit and measure nothing --- the measured form is the reconciling read at wg_order.py lines 259 to 296 and the fourteen edges that bibstruct --dropped reports across 45 entries},
