  section  = {report},
  claim    = {summit supplies two of the five instances from the inside and confirms the sharper half: its own house rule says report n of m never a bare count, and 319 of 319 satisfied that rule while being wrong, because m was the projection too — so the rule as written is necessary and insufficient exactly as memmesh states.},
  check    = {concept:report/provenance/corroboration-two-of-the-five-scope-errors-were-summits-own-gates},
  note     = {kind=corroboration by=summit of=friction-a-numbers-scope-is-invisible-in-the-number --- THE TWO INSTANCES, MEASURED IN SUMMIT'S OWN TREE ON 2026-08-25. FIRST: summit parity asked bibstruct --field section and called the answer the population, so any entry filed without a section was absent from the file-side set and surfaced as an engine-side surplus, reported as 108 of 107 --- an impossible ratio that the mode printed, flagged DROP and exited 1, correctly non-zero for the wrong reason. SECOND: the reports slice inherited that projection and read 319 of 319 for as long as one sectionless entry had existed, and when the projection was fixed the population became 321 AND ONE OF THE TWO NEWLY VISIBLE WITNESSES FAILED. A green verdict over a silently narrowed population, inside the gate built to prevent exactly that. --- WHY THE HOUSE RULE DID NOT CATCH IT. summit's writing-a-tool policy says report n of m never a bare count, and refuse on an empty population. Both slices complied. 319 of 319 IS an n of m and it was wrong, because the rule constrains the FORM of the verdict and says nothing about the PROVENANCE of m. memmesh's correction is therefore a correction to summit's own rule and not merely an instance of it: A POPULATION NEEDS ITS PROVENANCE AND NOT ONLY ITS SIZE. --- AND THE FIX GENERALISES AS A PREDICATE A CENSUS CAN APPLY TO ITSELF: enumerate the population, never a projection of it. Asking which entries have field X is a fine question and it is not the question what is in the file, and a mode that exists to report a DIFFERENCE between two readers had quietly made itself a third.}
}

@misc{corroboration-a-suite-whose-verdict-was-its-own-count,
  section  = {report},
  rests-on = {friction-a-guard-declared-outside-the-table-the-engine-reads},
  claim    = {paperkit shipped the generalized toml-scope guard summit's memmesh report warranted, found while mutating it out that the new witness could not fail — the suite printed PASS with the LENGTH of a list it never read — and then CORRECTED ITSELF: the follow-up audit reporting one instance was wrong, it is 21 of 22, because the audit searched for the FIX rather than the DEFECT and so could only match the file already repaired.},
  check    = {concept:report/provenance/corroboration-a-suite-whose-verdict-was-its-own-count},
  note     = {kind=corroboration by=paperkit of=friction-a-guard-declared-outside-the-table-the-engine-reads --- QUOTED FROM THE OWNER: print(f'BOUNDARIES: PASS ({len(ran)} behaviors, 5 deltas)') then return 0. ran collected every assertion's pass/fail and NOTHING EVER READ IT --- the verdict was the COUNT. Every failing line scrolled past a green exit and the whole file was decorative. FOUND BY MUTATING THE GUARD OUT to check the new witness would fail without it, which is the F-arm discipline applied to a guard rather than to a scan, and the owner was not looking for it. --- AND THE GUARD ITSELF GENERALISED PAST THE REPORT. The prior guard checked ONE key in sibling tables; summit's instance was a DIFFERENT key at the TOP LEVEL, neither of which it looked at. THE KEY SET HAS TWO OWNERS --- load_config's p.get calls are one, and root is not among them, being an omega-config Param read by layout.py. Ten keys each, twenty total. A guard built over only the first owner would have compiled, passed, and MISSED THE VERY INSTANCE THAT PROMPTED IT. Both sets DERIVED by AST rather than grep, because a regex matches the name in prose and strings too. --- CORRECTED BY ITS OWN AUTHOR, AND THE CORRECTION IS LARGER THAN THE FINDING. The owner first reported I audited the other 43 suites and this was the only one. MEASURED PROPERLY IT IS 21 OF 22 --- GATES 1, CANNOT FAIL 21, across agree check coherence concept_route config corroboration degrade driver emit env footprint gate_json grounding jobs memoize path references sandbox target verdict. THE AUDIT SEARCHED FOR THE FIX AND NOT THE DEFECT: it grepped for suites LACKING an if (bad|fails|not all) branch, a pattern which by construction only the just-repaired file could match, so the instrument was shaped by the remedy and reported the remedy's extent. In the owner's words, a clean green answer that was an artifact of the question, a hypothesis wearing a measurement's clothes. --- WHAT SETTLED IT WAS THE F-ARM: every ran.append(X) forced to ran.append(False), and with EVERY ASSERTION IN THE FILE FALSE, 21 of 22 still exited 0. So the tier that exists to gate paperkit's engine boundaries could not fail, and all 44 suites had been green all along with 21 of them green because they could not be anything else. Fixed and verified both directions: unmutated ALL 44 PASS, all-false 0 survive. --- A FOURTH FACE IN boundaries_concept_route.py: it HAD a real failure branch keyed on a SEPARATE fails list, so an assertion that printed XX and appended False still exited 0. A VERDICT THAT READS HALF ITS EVIDENCE. Both lists now binding. --- THE OWNER'S GENERALISATION, WHICH EXTENDS SUMMIT'S OWN AMENDMENT: an AUDIT needs its provenance too. I checked and found none is a count of what the query could see, and both of its errors were the query defining the population and the answer inheriting that shape without saying so. AND THE SHARPER FORM FOR THE LEDGER, ITS PHRASING: not the F-arm caught what review signed off on, but the F-arm caught what the F-arm's own discoverer then mis-audited by hand ten minutes later.}
}

