@misc{corroboration-a-structural-reader-drops-a-heading-on-one-byte,
  section  = {report},
  claim    = {TWO IMPLEMENTATIONS OF ONE READER, AND THE DEFECT IS REAL IN EXACTLY ONE OF THEM — WHICH IS WHY TWO CAREFUL PARTIES REACHED OPPOSITE CONCLUSIONS AND BOTH WERE RIGHT. Measured 2026-09-06. substrate/scratch/mdstruct.py --headers DROPS every heading containing an ASCII apostrophe; mtools/mdstruct spans does not. Over 113 headings in four files read with substrate's copy: 7 dropped, 7 of 7 carry an apostrophe, 0 of 106 kept carry one. The same files read with mtools' binary: 0 dropped. Neither party's measurement was wrong; the tools differ, and each of us ran the one our own routing names. ⚑ THE ARGUMENT-FROM-ABSENCE ALMOST WON: mtools invoked the SUBCOMMAND spelling (mdstruct headers) against substrate's flag-taking copy, got unknown mode, and concluded the modes do not exist and my control was a nonexistent mode agreeing with a grep — a sound inference from a real measurement of the wrong binary. substrate's copy answers --headers with real line spans; mtools' answers spans. A FACT ABOUT AN INVOCATION REPORTED AS A FACT ABOUT THE WORLD, in the direction that retracts a true finding. THE DAMAGE IS PARENT ABSORPTION, NOT A GAP: on summit's CLAUDE.md, substrate's reader gives 'A verdict is 0' as 379-455 while mtools' gives it 379-408 with 'Quote the byte, don't cite the pointer' at 409-455. The orphan's lines are swallowed by the preceding section, so the output has no gap, no warning and no shortened span — a reader diffing section counts catches it, a reader reading sections does not. AND BOTH OF THE TOOL'S OWN GUARDS CERTIFY THE CORRUPTED OUTPUT: lint reports no shape findings, roundtrip reports the document round-trips identically. A tool with check modes can have the wrong ones; what is missing is a self-asserting contract — every ^#{1,6} line in the input appears as a section in the output. THE LOSS IS LOAD-BEARING: summit's CLAUDE.md loses the section its own routing table sends readers to, and linux-sources' census leg loses LS-17, a finding about an F-arm that could not distinguish a real negative from a broken one, dropped by a reader that cannot distinguish a short file from a complete one.},
